d from getting  away but before the rebel soldiers can  escape to their doctor the Tantive for  the hatch jams the lights in this tunnel  cut out the soldiers turn at the sound  of another hatch the opposite end  closing and then another sound under the  sounds of the sirens the breaths of  Darth Vader  the director of this film Gareth Edwards  focuses on these soldiers because  they're goofy helmets and faces a fear  perfectly matched the opening imagery of  the 1977 Star Wars directors telling us  that in these next few seconds we are  going to learn exactly what those  soldiers were afraid of and incite us he  creates the dread of dramatic irony  because we know from that 1977 opening  scene that none of these rebel soldiers  will survive and the point of view of  these soldiers returns later to the  mysterious space sorcerer that people  remove from the Skywalker saga CMS not  the complex tragic hero that we saw him  as in the prequels but now once again  the pure elemental evil that made us wet  ourselves as kids the darkness suddenly  illuminates red from the blade of  Vader's lightsaber now up until this  point  rogue 1 was the first Star Wars film not  to show a lightsaber because this story  was focused on the ground soldiers of  the Galactic Civil War not the Arthurian  Skywalker mythology of the rest of the  series but this red lightsaber severs  the final minutes of this film from that  war story drawing a line to mark the  transition back into the main Star Wars  narrative to remind us of the stakes of  this mission and the red lightsaber is  the primary source of light in this  scene the color red evokes emergency  lights the hell that Vader is now  trapped these rebel soldiers in and the  literal bloodbath of these casualties  now before rogue one we had obviously  seen Vader's violence on screen plenty  of times he choked rebels with his hands  he forced choked Admiral mati he engaged  Luke and obi-wan and epic lightsaber  duels  he used the force to block blaster bolts  and even gank blasters out of people's  hands and here in less than 60 seconds  Vader pulls off a highlight reel of all  of these past moves he effortlessly  swings his lightsaber as he did against  Luke and Empire he deflects blaster  bolts and force pulls blasters out of  hands like he did against Han Solo and  Cloud City he flees rebel bodies against  the walls and ceiling forced choking  them exactly as he did to Admiral mati  and bridging together all of these  visual callbacks is a raw ferocity from  Vader that was always under the mask  actually that first rebel that he forced  lifts to the ceiling Vader passes  to him with his fist still clenched  holding him up there but the clenched  fist also forms a menacing gesture to  the rebel over there at the hatch as if  to say how dare you try to defy the  Empire and without breaking eye contact  Vader flings his blade behind his back  slashing the lifted rebel meanwhile the  soldier with the data card desperately  reaches through the hatch opening with  Vader's saber framed behind him slashing  closer and closer the sequence is edited  with the claustrophobia of a horror film  here we see a screaming victim fleeing a  killer through an impossibly narrow gap  to freedom actually later when this  rebel does pass the card through the  second rebel trips just like every  fleeing horror film victim headed in Lee  does sure it's a cliche but it does make  sense because when you're that scared  you lose your balance you become a klutz  it's like when you have a nightmare and  you try to run in your dream it's really  difficult because your body in real life  is asleep so it's like you're paralyzed  so the rebel who hands off the data card  gets gored at the last second the saber  pierces the door and then the hatch  opens to let Vader through and that is  one of the two great hidden details of  this sequence the reason this hatch was  jammed the reason they couldn't pry it  open the reason all of those rebels were  trapped in that hallway for the  slaughter Vader was holding it closed  the whole time a long time now the other  great detail about the sequence is the  music rogue one was composed by Michael  Giacchino  who chose to be more discreet with his  references to John Williams classic  themes the music here is a dramatic  choral chant if you're familiar with the  composition of John Williams Star Wars  themes they're known for their brass  instruments carrying the melody like the  French horn of the forest theme the  trumpet of the main Star Wars theme one  memorable exception was Williams duel  the fates theme from The Phantom Menace  which of course used a chorus human  voices Williams did that to give the  duel a religious temple-like feel  chiquinho does a similar effect here  with Vader as a spiritual force bringing  him full circle with his first  appearance in the film in his Sith  temple also the chorus voices blend with  the screams of Terror from the rebel  soldiers as if each step of Vader's  Death March unleashes a shriek of agony  but most impressively the music still  feels familiar  and part of Vader's DNA because  chiquinho sneekly based it on John  Williams music if you speed it up you  can hear how it's actually the Imperial  March theme look at my opinion rogue one  was a much better film than people have  given it credit for and honestly the  last Star Wars film that made me jump to  my feet in the plaza the closing credits  and this Darth Vader sequence is exactly  why it restored the best villain of  Hollywood cinema to his iconic  silhouette and it allowed that shadow to  show off with the horror that made him  the ghost story of the Star Wars  universe comment down below with your  favorite Darth Vader moment and follow  me on instagram and twitter at EA boss  it subscribed a new rock stars for more  Star Wars analysis breakdowns for all  the updates for upcoming Star Wars  properties thank you for joining me and  yep wet myself